---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Woofer from '../components/Woofer.astro';
import TrendPipeline from '../components/TrendPipeline.astro';
import FounderAvatar from '../components/FounderAvatar.astro';

const sounds = await getCollection('sounds');
const categories = await getCollection('categories');
const posts = await getCollection('blog');
---
<Base
  title="About Bleepboard"
  description="Bleepboard is a free meme soundboard: instant sound buttons, free MP3 downloads, and the stories behind the internet's favorite sounds."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="tick"></span>About us</p>
    <h1>About Bleepboard</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Bleepboard is a soundboard built around one idea: when a meme takes off, you should be
      able to play the sound — and know where it came from — within seconds.
    </p>
    <ul class="stats">
      <li><b class="tnum">{sounds.length}</b> sound buttons</li>
      <li><b class="tnum">{categories.length}</b> soundboards</li>
      <li><b class="tnum">{posts.length}</b> explainers</li>
      <li><b>Free</b> forever</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 1. how a sound gets found ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="pipeline">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>How a sound gets found</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      Half machine, half stubborn human. Here is the actual route from "the internet is
      making a noise" to "there is a button for it".
    </p>

    <div class="foreman">
      <Woofer size={96} decorative />
      <p>
        Woofer, our mascot, gets the credit for this bit. The rest of us do the typing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TrendPipeline />
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 2. origin story ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="why">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Why we built this</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Meme sounds have always been easy to find and impossible to understand. You could get
        the clip in about four clicks from a dozen sites — under a banner ad, behind a popup,
        next to a download button that wasn't the download button. What you couldn't get was
        an answer to the obvious question: <strong>what is this, actually?</strong> Which film,
        which stream, which unhinged six seconds of someone's Tuesday. The clip arrived with no
        label, no context and no reason to be funny beyond "it just is".
      </p>
      <p>
        That gap is the whole reason Bleepboard exists. We wanted the soundboard that explains
        itself — where pressing the button is the fast part and the story is right underneath it.
      </p>

      <h3>What that means in practice</h3>
      <ul class="why-list">
        <li>
          <b>Every sound has a write-up, written by a person.</b> Not a templated sentence with
          the title swapped in. The site's own content schema enforces a minimum length on those
          descriptions, so a sound with a thin or empty write-up fails the build rather than
          quietly shipping. It is a deliberately annoying rule and we have kept it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>{categories.length} curated soundboards, not one undifferentiated dump.</b> Sounds
          live in hubs — meme, gaming, Discord, brainrot, SpongeBob and the rest — each with its
          own written introduction, so browsing gets you somewhere instead of scrolling forever.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>{posts.length} long-form explainers.</b> Some sounds have a history too big for a
          paragraph, so they get a <a href="/blog/">full article</a> on where the thing came from
          and how it escaped into everyone's For You page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>No signup, no install, no wall.</b> Buttons play the moment you tap them, every
          clip downloads free as an MP3, and nothing on this site asks you to make an account
          first.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        None of that is clever engineering. It's just the boring, unscalable work of listening
        to a clip and writing down what it is — which is exactly why so few soundboards bother.
      </p>
      <p>
        Want a sound added? Found something that shouldn't be here? See our
        <a href="/dmca/">DMCA &amp; takedown page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 3. founder ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="founder">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="author-card">
      <span class="founder-portrait">
        <FounderAvatar size={128} />
      </span>
      <div class="author-body">
        <h3>Zam Zam Gillani</h3>
        <p class="author-role">Founder</p>
        {/* TODO: replace with Zam Zam's own words */}
        <p>
          Zam Zam founded Bleepboard on a narrow, slightly pedantic conviction: a sound button
          should be able to tell you what it is. Plenty of soundboards will hand you the audio.
          Almost none will tell you which film, stream or late-night upload it escaped from.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the rule here is hers — nothing gets a button until someone has worked out where it
          came from and written that down. It is the slowest possible way to run a soundboard,
          and it is the only version worth having.
        </p>
        <p class="author-note">
          The portrait above is an illustration in the site's house style, not a photograph.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/trending/">Play trending sounds</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/categories/">Browse soundboards</a>
    </p>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  /* Woofer introducing the pipeline. Kept small — he's a caption, not a hero. */
  .foreman {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s4);
    margin: var(--s4) 0 var(--s2);
  }
  .foreman p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 44ch;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  @media (max-width: 559px) {
    .foreman :global(.woofer) { width: 72px; height: 72px; }
  }

  /* "What that means in practice" — the receipts for the paragraph above it. */
  .why-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--s4) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s3);
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .why-list { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }
  .why-list li {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    padding: var(--s4);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .why-list b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 750; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px; }

  /* .why-list breaks out of the prose measure — the two-up grid needs the room. */
  .prose .why-list { max-width: none; }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    #why .prose { max-width: 78ch; }
  }

  /* The avatar draws its own rounded tile (rx scales with the SVG), so the
     wrapper stays shapeless — a fixed border-radius here would fight it. */
  .founder-portrait {
    flex: none;
    display: block;
    line-height: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 559px) {
    .founder-portrait :global(.founder-avatar) { width: 88px; height: 88px; }
  }
</style>
